<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Act of Independence of Lithuania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Act of 16 February</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Act of Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> on 16 February 1918 by the twenty members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a>, chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Antanas Smetona</a>. It proclaimed an independent, democratic Lithuanian state with Vilnius as its capital, severing all earlier ties with other nations. Although German troops still occupied the country, the date is celebrated as Lithuania's State Restoration Day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/act-of-independence-of-lithuania.jpg" alt="Act of Independence of Lithuania" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Council of Lithuania · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Original_Act_of_Independence_of_Lithuania_hand-written_in_Lithuanian_language.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> had a centuries-long tradition of statehood going back to the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a>. After the last Partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in 1795 it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> rebelled in 1830 and 1863. Their first real opportunity came when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> were weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. After the Russian Revolution of 1917 Germany allowed the Vilnius Conference, hoping that it would proclaim a closer relationship with Germany. Held on 18–23 September 1917, the conference instead resolved that an independent Lithuania should be established, with any closer relationship conditional on German recognition, and it elected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a> of twenty members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> pressed the Council to form an alliance. The Act of 11 December 1917, drafted first by the German foreign ministry, proclaimed independence but asked for German protection and a firm and permanent alliance; it was adopted by fifteen votes to three, with one abstention. On 8 January 1918 the Council amended the text, adding democratic principles and a Constituent Assembly and omitting the alliance, but the German military administration rejected the changes, and four members resigned in protest.</p><p>On 16 February 1918 all twenty members met at 30 Didžioji Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, a building now called the House of the Signatories. The Act proclaimed the restoration of an independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, governed by democratic principles, with Vilnius as its capital, and ended the ties binding Lithuania to other states. It was approved unanimously, though the four who had resigned were uneasy that the Constituent Assembly would decide Lithuania's foundation and foreign relations finally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>German newspapers printed the text on 18 February, but German authorities forbade its publication in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and the editor Petras Klimas hid about 60 copies of the Council's newspaper, Lietuvos aidas. Little changed until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> lost the war; on 2 November 1918 the Council adopted a provisional constitution.</p><p>The Act is the legal basis of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, in the interwar period and since 1990, when the Act of 11 March 1990 stated that it had never lost its legal effect. The original disappeared after 15 June 1940, when Lithuania received a Soviet ultimatum, and was reported found in the political archive of the German foreign office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in March 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Act of Independence of Lithuania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A conference in Vilnius in September 1917 elected a twenty-member Council of Lithuania, the Taryba, chaired by Antanas Smetona, and on 16 February 1918 its members, among them Jonas Basanavičius, signed the <b>Act of Independence</b>, proclaiming an independent democratic state with Vilnius as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
